task: dataflow-wire
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-wire
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfw
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 4 (first half)

why: the engine is called distributed and is entirely in-process.
  Nothing crosses a machine boundary, and stage 4 is what makes the
  word true. Its load-bearing question is not sockets — okay-cluster
  has had those since P7 — it is WHAT crosses: a partition's partial
  result, described by a Schema, because Claim 3 says no closure ever
  travels and no Java serialization is used.

what, in two halves so each lands whole:
  THIS LANE splits `Sink`'s two conflated ideas. `P` is the
  partition's WORKING state — a live `Windows`, mutable maps, a
  terminal accumulator being folded into. `W` is what LEAVES the
  partition, and only W has to be a value: immutable, mergeable and
  codec-able. `finish(p): W` closes the operator and hands it over;
  `result`, `drops` and `merged` take W.

  Then `Wire[A, R] extends Sink[A, R]` adds one thing — a
  `Schema[W]` — and composes through `and` exactly as `Sink` does.

  Acceptance without a socket: a fan whose every partial is ENCODED
  and DECODED between `finish` and `result` answers what the local
  run answers, at every parallelism. A wire that changes the answer
  is caught here, in milliseconds, rather than across four processes.

  NEXT LANE (dataflow-workers) is the coordinator and the worker
  processes, with the Wrocław checksums across four of them.

note on what this buys beyond the wire: with the completeness rule a
  partition has already folded most of its panes into its terminal
  accumulator, so what crosses is a SUMMARY plus the boundary panes —
  map-side aggregation carried all the way to the terminal. The
  Schema requirement is on that summary, which is the smallest thing
  it could be on.

predict: the split will be mechanical except for the windowed sink,
  where `Panes` currently hides three different things behind one
  type — and I expect the split to make the completeness rule easier
  to read rather than harder, since "finished locally" and "handed
  over" stop being fields of the same object.
